WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.431
This lesson becomes much easier when the learner stops using simple harmonic motion as a decorative label.

2
00:00:06.431 --> 00:00:12.862
SHM is the special case where the restoring force, and therefore the acceleration, is proportional to displacement and directed back toward equilibrium.

3
00:00:12.862 --> 00:00:19.292
That sign matters.

4
00:00:19.292 --> 00:00:25.723
If the object is to the right, the acceleration points left.

5
00:00:25.723 --> 00:00:32.154
If the object is to the left, the acceleration points right.

6
00:00:32.154 --> 00:00:38.585
The rule is powerful because it predicts both size and direction at once.

7
00:00:38.585 --> 00:00:45.016
Once the learner trusts that structure, the worked example becomes a sign-aware translation task rather than a memorized chant about back-and-forth motion.

8
00:00:45.016 --> 00:00:51.447
Keep this takeaway in view: SHM becomes readable once the learner treats the sign and the proportionality as the defining structure.

9
00:00:51.447 --> 00:00:57.878
SHM means special case.

10
00:00:57.878 --> 00:01:04.308
Not every oscillation is SHM; the proportional restoring rule is what makes this case special.

11
00:01:04.308 --> 00:01:10.739
Sign means points inward.

12
00:01:10.739 --> 00:01:17.170
The minus sign in the SHM law keeps the acceleration aimed back toward equilibrium.

13
00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:23.718
This diagram matters because it shows the SHM law as a living pattern rather than as a line of symbols.

14
00:01:23.718 --> 00:01:30.266
On one side of equilibrium, the restoring acceleration points back one way.

15
00:01:30.266 --> 00:01:36.813
On the other side, it points back the opposite way.

16
00:01:36.813 --> 00:01:43.361
The magnitude also grows when the displacement grows.

17
00:01:43.361 --> 00:01:49.909
That means the learner can read the sign and the proportionality from the picture before calculating anything.

18
00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:56.457
This is exactly the habit the worked example needs.

19
00:01:56.457 --> 00:02:03.004
Keep this takeaway in view: The SHM diagram works because it turns sign and proportionality into visible commitments before algebra starts.

20
00:02:03.004 --> 00:02:09.552
Right side: Positive displacement must produce an inward, leftward restoring acceleration.

21
00:02:09.552 --> 00:02:16.100
Left side: Negative displacement must produce an inward, rightward restoring acceleration.

22
00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:22.007
A spring trolley on a track is a good analogy because the return pull grows as the trolley is dragged farther from the center point.

23
00:02:22.007 --> 00:02:27.915
If the trolley is displaced to the right, the tether pulls left.

24
00:02:27.915 --> 00:02:33.822
If the trolley is displaced to the left, the tether pulls right.

25
00:02:33.822 --> 00:02:39.729
The farther away it goes, the stronger that return tendency becomes.

26
00:02:39.729 --> 00:02:45.636
That captures the central SHM idea very well: opposite direction, proportional size.

27
00:02:45.636 --> 00:02:51.544
The analogy is not the full mathematics, but it gives the learner an intuition that matches the law.

28
00:02:51.544 --> 00:02:57.451
Keep this takeaway in view: The spring-trolley analogy makes the SHM law feel like a disciplined return rule with both sign and size.

29
00:02:57.451 --> 00:03:03.358
Center post means equilibrium.

30
00:03:03.358 --> 00:03:09.265
The trolley is always being referenced back to one central balance position.

31
00:03:09.265 --> 00:03:15.173
Farther pull means stronger return.

32
00:03:15.173 --> 00:03:21.080
Greater displacement produces a greater restoring response in the same system.

33
00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:26.354
The key equations are the restoring-force law and the acceleration law built from it.

34
00:03:26.354 --> 00:03:31.628
The force relation is F equals negative k times x.

35
00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:36.902
Dividing by mass gives a equals negative k over m times x.

36
00:03:36.902 --> 00:03:42.176
These relations matter because they keep three ideas together.

37
00:03:42.176 --> 00:03:47.450
The sign points inward.

38
00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:52.724
The size grows with displacement magnitude.

39
00:03:52.724 --> 00:03:57.998
And the same system keeps the same k over m factor every time.

40
00:03:57.998 --> 00:04:03.272
Once that is secure, the worked example becomes a clean translation from position to acceleration.

41
00:04:03.272 --> 00:04:08.546
Keep this takeaway in view: The SHM equations become easy to trust once the learner keeps the sign, the scaling, and the system factor together.

42
00:04:08.546 --> 00:04:13.820
Use x with sign: A right-hand displacement and a left-hand displacement should not be entered with the same sign.

43
00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:19.094
Force then acceleration: The worked example often feels cleaner if force is found first and acceleration second.

44
00:04:19.094 --> 00:04:24.368
F = -kx.

45
00:04:24.368 --> 00:04:29.642
a = -(k/m)x.

46
00:04:29.642 --> 00:04:34.916
The minus sign is not decoration.

47
00:04:34.916 --> 00:04:40.190
It tells the learner that the restoring response points back toward equilibrium.

48
00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:47.292
A strong SHM routine is to assign the sign of the displacement first, apply the restoring relation second, and divide by the mass third if acceleration is required.

49
00:04:47.292 --> 00:04:54.394
After that, compare magnitudes only if the question asks about proportionality.

50
00:04:54.394 --> 00:05:01.497
This order matters because learners often jump straight to a positive number and then try to fix the direction in words afterward.

51
00:05:01.497 --> 00:05:08.599
The safer route is sign first, relation second, acceleration third, comparison last.

52
00:05:08.599 --> 00:05:15.701
Keep this takeaway in view: Sign-scale-divide is the safest SHM routine because it protects both direction and proportionality.

53
00:05:15.701 --> 00:05:22.803
Step 1 means set the sign.

54
00:05:22.803 --> 00:05:29.906
Choose whether the displacement is positive or negative from its side of equilibrium.

55
00:05:29.906 --> 00:05:37.008
Step 2 means apply the restoring law.

56
00:05:37.008 --> 00:05:44.110
Use the negative sign in the equation to point the force and acceleration back inward.

57
00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:48.480
Try four checks.

58
00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.850
Does SHM require the restoring acceleration to be proportional to displacement?

59
00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:57.220
Yes.

60
00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:01.590
Does the restoring acceleration point away from equilibrium?

61
00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:05.960
No, it points toward equilibrium.

62
00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:10.330
If the displacement changes from positive to negative, must the acceleration sign also change?

63
00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:14.700
Yes.

64
00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:19.070
And in the same mass-spring system, does halving the displacement halve the acceleration magnitude?

65
00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:23.440
Yes.

66
00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:27.810
Those checks show whether the real SHM condition is in place.

67
00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:32.180
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can defend the SHM sign rule and the proportional scaling together.

68
00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:36.550
Definition means proportional and inward.

69
00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:40.920
Both the scaling and the direction are part of SHM, not only the repetition.

70
00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:45.290
Sign flip means crossing equilibrium.

71
00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:49.660
Changing the sign of x changes the sign of the restoring acceleration.

72
00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:56.102
You are ready for the worked example now.

73
00:06:56.102 --> 00:07:02.544
Start by deciding the sign of the displacement from the side of equilibrium.

74
00:07:02.544 --> 00:07:08.985
Then apply the restoring-force law or the acceleration form of the same rule.

75
00:07:08.985 --> 00:07:15.427
Then compare any second displacement only after the first sign-aware result is secure.

76
00:07:15.427 --> 00:07:21.869
The best answers in this lesson sound like one continuous chain from position, to restoring response, to interpretation.

77
00:07:21.869 --> 00:07:28.311
Keep this takeaway in view: The SHM worked example becomes much safer when the learner treats position, sign, and restoring acceleration as one chain.

78
00:07:28.311 --> 00:07:34.753
Use symbols honestly: Treat x as a signed quantity instead of hiding the side of equilibrium in words only.

79
00:07:34.753 --> 00:07:41.195
Keep the system fixed: The proportional comparison only works cleanly when the same spring and mass are being discussed.

80
00:07:41.195 --> 00:07:47.636
assign the displacement sign from the position.

81
00:07:47.636 --> 00:07:54.078
apply F = -kx or a = -(k/m)x with the sign still visible.

82
00:07:54.078 --> 00:08:00.520
A strong solution keeps the inward-direction logic visible in every line, not only in the final sentence.
